{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ spacecraft space probe rover) (+ power electricity batteries generator))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#0_350339518","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q963","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#0_350339518\" , \"end_char\": 1621.000000 , \"headings\": \"Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Explore further \" , \"segment\": \"Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars July 29, 2020<cover>Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars by Oak Ridge National Laboratory The Perseverance rover, </cover>headed for Mars this summer, carries a plutonium isotope produced at ORNL, the first domestic production in about 30 years. As it decays, Pu-238 will power the rover and its instruments across the planet. Credit: NASA After its long journey to Mars beginning this summer, NASA's Perseverance rover will be powered across the planet's surface in part by plutonium produced at the Department of Energy's Oak Ridge National Laboratory. \"Mars 2020 will be the first NASA mission that uses ORNL-produced plutonium-238,\" said Alan Icenhour, associate laboratory director for nuclear science and engineering at ORNL. \" This accomplishment represents countless hours of work by dedicated ORNL staff, and it's rewarding to see "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"1/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#0_350339518","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to plutonium-238 powering the Perseverance Mars rover and directly explains that Pu-238 decays to power the rover and its instruments, a clear example of plutonium improving space technology.","learned":"Plutonium-238 radioisotope power enables Mars rover operation by providing power for mobility and instruments."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"The current Pu-238 spacecraft power query produced a highly relevant result; continue to gather similar direct evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_907837353#6_1918839783","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":2,"snippet":"28_907837353#6_1918839783\" , \"end_char\": 6831.000000 , \"headings\": \"Uranus, Neptune in NASA’s sights for new robotic mission Uranus, Neptune in NASA’s sights for new robotic mission\" , \"segment\": \"NASA/NRC Decadal Survey Orbiters for Uranus and Neptune will almost certainly rely on nuclear batteries powered by plutonium, and U.S. government funding of additional production of space-grade plutonium ensures such projects will have the power resources they need, Green said. Engineers pack pellets of <cover>plutonium-238, the isotope tailored for electricity in space, </cover>into radioisotope generators. The natural decay of plutonium-238 produces heat, which is transferred through thermoelectric couples to generate electricity. “It would be after 2023, as we get into the next decade that we would consider using radioisotope power for those missions, if they survive the decadal process, which I’m sure they will,” Green said Monday. Nuclear power is required for probes in the far reaches of the solar system, where sunlight is insufficient for solar arrays. NASA’s New Horizons mission to Pluto, the Cassini mission at Saturn and the Curiosity rover on Mars "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"2/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_28_907837353#6_1918839783","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that plutonium-238 is tailored for electricity in space, that its decay heat is converted to electricity in radioisotope generators, and that such nuclear power is required for distant probes where solar power is insufficient.","learned":"Pu-238 improves space exploration technology by enabling nuclear batteries/radioisotope generators for deep-space probes and rovers when sunlight is too weak."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"This query is yielding strong direct evidence about Pu-238 powering spacecraft; continue through the result list for more grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#1_350341527","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":3,"snippet":"1_350341527\" , \"end_char\": 2039.000000 , \"headings\": \"Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Explore further \" , \"segment\": \"This accomplishment represents countless hours of work by dedicated ORNL staff, and it's rewarding to see this work come to fruition. Helping NASA in its mission to Mars is a significant moment in the history of the lab.\\\" Like other rovers on deep-<cover>space missions, Perseverance's traveling power on Mars comes from thermoelectric generators that create electricity from heat generated from the decay of plutonium-238 </cover>in the form of oxide ceramic pellets. Pu-238 produces heat as it decays, and the rover's multi-mission radioisotope thermoelectric generator converts that heat into electricity to charge the lithium-ion batteries that move the rover and power the instruments it will use on the surface of the Red Planet. Pu-238 is ideal for deep-space travel because of its long half-life of nearly 88 years, but it has been in short supply. Previously, the U.S. stockpile consisted primarily of Pu-238 produced "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"3/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#1_350341527","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the ORNL Perseverance article and directly states that thermoelectric generators convert heat from plutonium-238 decay into electricity to charge batteries and power rover instruments.","learned":"Pu-238's long half-life and decay heat make it ideal for deep-space travel, powering rover batteries and instruments through radioisotope thermoelectric generators."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"The current query continues to return direct grade-3 evidence about plutonium improving space and rover technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_1306202651#3_2673088012","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":4,"snippet":"very harsh conditions, particularly where solar power is not feasible. The importance of such power sources was illustrated by the European Space Agency's Rosetta mission, which successfully landed the Philae probe on comet 67P/Churymov–Gerasimenko in 2014. Equipped with batteries and solar panels, the position in which Philae came to rest on the comet's surface – shielded from the Sun's rays by cliffs – meant that the lander was unable to make use of solar energy and was only able to send 64 hours' worth of data before its battery power ran out. So far over 45 RTGs have powered in excess of 25 US space vehicles including Apollo, Pioneer, Viking, Voyager, Galileo, Ulysses, Cassini and New Horizons space missions as well as many civil and military satellites. The Cassini <cover>spacecraft carried three RTGs providing 870 watts of power from 33 kg plutonium-238 </cover>oxide as it explored Saturn.\" , \"start_char\": 2661.000000 , \"title\": \"Nuclear Reactors for Space - World Nuclear Association\" , \"url\": \"https://world-nuclear.org/information-library/non-power-nuclear-applications/transport/nuclear-reactors-for-space.aspx\" }"}}
