{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_31_1306202651#3_2673088012","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is about nuclear power for space and directly states that RTGs have powered many space vehicles and that Cassini used plutonium-238 oxide to provide electrical power while exploring Saturn.","learned":"Plutonium-238 improves space technology by supplying reliable RTG power for missions such as Apollo, Voyager, Cassini, and New Horizons, especially where solar power is unreliable or infeasible."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"The Pu-238 spacecraft power query is still producing strong grade-3 evidence; continue to collect additional direct supporting documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#7_2118928048","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":5,"snippet":"Why does NASA need more Pu-238? How is Pu-238 made? What is INL’s role? Who is funding the production of Pu-238? Multimedia Gallery MARS 2020 ROVER by Marco_Romero \" , \"segment\": \"This spring it was shipped directly from INL to the launch site at Kennedy Space Center Florida for integration into the rover. MMRTGs work by converting heat from the natural decay of radioisotope materials into electricity. The generators consist of two major elements: a heat source that contains plutonium-238 (Pu-<cover>238) and thermocouples that convert the plutonium’s decay heat energy and the cold of space to electricity. </cover>The MMRTG is designed to produce about 110 watts of electrical power to begin the mission. The system has a design life of 17 years, but can be expected to produce power much longer than that. Additionally, the MMRTG provides a source of heat for the rover’s instruments and on-board systems in the cold environment. Thermocouples have no moving parts and have proved an amazingly reliable source of energy for space missions. More Info TIMELINE This timeline provides information on each milestone for the Mars 2020 Multi-Mission Radioisotope "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"5/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#7_2118928048","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that MMRTGs use a plutonium-238 heat source and thermocouples to convert decay heat into electricity, producing reliable electrical power and heat for the Mars 2020 rover and its instruments.","learned":"Pu-238 improves rover and spacecraft technology through MMRTGs that provide long-lived electricity and thermal support without moving parts."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"The current query is highly productive for grade-3 evidence on plutonium-238 enabling space power systems; continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q963"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q963","rank":6,"snippet":"and delivers the power source to NASA. More Info WHAT IS AN MMRTG, AND HOW DOES IT WORK? Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators are ideal for space missions because they are compact, durable and reliable, providing continuous power over long periods of time. The Department of Energy provides radioisotope power systems to NASA for civil space applications. The MMRTG for the Mars 2020 mission was fueled and tested at the DOE’s Idaho National Laboratory. This spring it was shipped directly from INL to the launch site at Kennedy Space Center Florida for integration into the rover. MMRTGs work by converting heat from the natural decay of radioisotope materials into electricity. The generators consist of two major elements: a heat source that contains plutonium-238 (Pu-<cover>238) and thermocouples that convert the plutonium’s decay heat energy and the cold of space to electricity. </cover>The MMRTG is designed to produce about 110 watts of electrical power to begin the mission.\" , \"start_char\": 4120.000000 , \"title\": \"Mars 2020 | Powering NASA’s Mars Perseverance Rover - INL\" , \"url\": \"https://inl.gov/mars-2020/\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"6/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","relevance":3,"reason":"This INL Mars 2020 page segment directly explains that MMRTGs are compact, durable, reliable space power systems using a plutonium-238 heat source and thermocouples to convert decay heat into electricity for the Perseverance rover.","learned":"A Pu-238 MMRTG provides continuous, long-lived electrical power for NASA rover technology; this is overlapping evidence from the same INL Mars 2020 source as the prior result."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q963","reason":"Although recent results include overlapping Mars 2020 evidence, the query has consistently returned grade-3 documents on plutonium-enabled space technology; check the next result for additional distinct evidence."}}
