{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","relevance":3,"reason":"The document directly explains that NASA's Mars 2020 rover uses an MMRTG/nuclear battery that converts heat from plutonium-238 decay into about 110 W of electricity, keeping instruments running and warm, and states such systems are compact, durable, reliable, and long-lasting for space missions.","learned":"Pu-238 improves space/rover technology by enabling long-lasting nuclear batteries that provide electricity and heat in harsh environments."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q964","reason":"The explicit RTG/NASA query produced a strong grade-3 result; continue for more distinct authoritative evidence about plutonium-powered technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q964"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#8_2118930959","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q964","rank":2,"snippet":"design life of 17 years, but can be expected to produce power much longer than that. Additionally, the MMRTG provides a source of heat for the rover’s instruments and on-board systems in the cold environment. Thermocouples have no moving parts and have proved an amazingly reliable source of energy for space missions. More Info TIMELINE This timeline provides information on each milestone for the Mars 2020 Multi-Mission <cover>Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator. Scroll through the timeline events below. HOT TOPICS National labs resume plutonium production for space exploration While the United States has enough plutonium to fuel the space missions scheduled for the next decade, the domestic supply of plutonium is a limited resource. In order to establish a new supply of Pu-238 to enable future NASA </cover>missions, Congress directed the Department of Energy (DOE) to resume Pu-238 production. Read More To Pluto and beyond: Work that reaches the stars They’ve stayed in the same spot for more than a decade, but their work has traveled to Pluto and beyond, and landed on the surface of Mars. Bob Gomez, Courtney Swassing and Jon Bradley have spent most of their Idaho National Laboratory (INL) careers in the "}}
