{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#8_2118930959","relevance":3,"reason":"This INL Mars 2020 power page segment directly discusses the Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator, its long design life, heat support for rover instruments, and the need for Pu-238 production to enable future NASA missions.","learned":"Pu-238 supports long-lived, reliable radioisotope generator technology for Mars rovers and future NASA missions, including power and heat for instruments."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q964","reason":"The RTG/NASA query is still producing direct grade-3 evidence on plutonium-enabled space power systems; continue to the next result."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q964"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#5_350347794","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q964","rank":3,"snippet":"Laboratory for fabrication into ceramic pellets for the thermoelectric generator. Leftover neptunium is recycled to make more Pu-238. Pause Play % buffered 00:00 00:00 Unmute Mute Disable captions Enable captions Settings Captions Disabled Quality 0 Speed Normal Captions Go back to previous menu Quality Go back to previous menu Speed Go back to previous menu 0.5× 0.75× Normal 1.25× 1.5× 1.75× 2× 4× PIP Exit fullscreen Enter fullscreen Play RNL produces some pretty out-of-this-world materials. <cover>Plutonium-238, a unique iridium alloy, and carbon-bonded carbon fiber are all key ingredients for deep space exploration. NASA uses these materials in the Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator, or MMRTG. An MMRTG is like a nuclear-powered battery pack that can sustain far-reaching space missions </cover>for decades. NASA's Perseverance mission to Mars is the latest to feature one of these generators outfitted with ORNL tech. Credit:\" , \"start_char\": 3089.000000 , \"title\": \"Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars\" , \"url\": \"https://phys.org/news/2020-07-plutonium-power-perseverance-mars.html\" }"}}
