{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#5_350347794","relevance":3,"reason":"The Phys.org/ORNL article segment directly states that plutonium-238 is a key ingredient in NASA's MMRTG nuclear-powered battery pack, which can sustain far-reaching space missions for decades, with Perseverance as an example.","learned":"Pu-238 improves deep-space exploration technology by serving as fuel for MMRTG nuclear battery systems that can power missions for decades."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q964","reason":"The RTG/NASA query continues to return direct grade-3 evidence; continue a bit further for additional distinct documents before diversifying."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q964"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#6_350349576","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q964","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#6_350349576\" , \"end_char\": 5144.000000 , \"headings\": \"Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Plutonium-238 to help power Perseverance on Mars Explore further \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Plutonium-238, a unique iridium alloy, and carbon-bonded carbon fiber are all key ingredients for deep space exploration. NASA uses these materials in the Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator, or MMRTG. An MMRTG is like a nuclear-powered battery pack that can sustain far-reaching space missions </cover>for decades. NASA's Perseverance mission to Mars is the latest to feature one of these generators outfitted with ORNL tech. Credit: Jenny Woodbery/ORNLAnimations courtesy of NASA, NASA's Jet Propulsion Laboratory and The Johns Hopkins University Applied Physics Laboratory. HFIR can irradiate up to 6,800 grams of neptunium per year in batches that stay in the reactor for two to three months. INL's Advanced Test Reactor is also irradiating small quantities of neptunium on a limited basis and is slated for a makeover next year that will increase its capability to produce "}}
