{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_26_167962818#6_350349576","relevance":3,"reason":"This is overlapping evidence from the same Phys.org/ORNL Perseverance article, but it directly states that plutonium-238 is a key ingredient in NASA's MMRTG nuclear-powered battery pack, which can sustain far-reaching space missions for decades.","learned":"Repeated evidence: Pu-238 enables long-duration deep-space mission technology through MMRTG nuclear battery packs."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ pacemaker pacemakers heart cardiac) (+ battery batteries power powered))","reason":"Diversify beyond spacecraft: plutonium-238 was used in long-lived nuclear batteries for cardiac pacemakers, another direct example of plutonium improving technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ pacemaker pacemakers heart cardiac) (+ battery batteries power powered))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_1245654658#3_2504437366","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q965","rank":1,"snippet":"Stations Heating Devices Coast Guard Buoys Oil Well Equipment Seismic and Oceanographic Devices \" , \"segment\": \"Smoke particles disrupt this current, triggering an alarm. Spacecraft Power Radioisotope thermoelectric generators are used to power a wide array of satellites and spacecraft, including Pioneer 10 and 11 and Voyager 1 and 2. These devices function like a battery, with the benefit of a long life span. Plutonium-238 serves as the fuel source, producing alpha radiation resulting in heat, which is converted to electricity. Pacemaker Battery Alpha radiation is used as an energy source to <cover>power heart pacemakers. Plutonium-238 </cover>is used as the fuel source for such batteries; with a half-life of 88 years, this source of power provides a long lifespan for pacemakers. However, due to their toxicity, difficulties with patients in traveling, and problems with disposal, they are no longer used. Remote Sensing Stations The Unites States Air Force uses alpha radiation to power remote sensing stations in Alaska. Strontium-90 is typically used as the fuel source.\" , \"start_char\": 1845.000000 , \"title\": \"10 Uses of Alpha Radiation | Sciencing\" , \""}}
{"request":{"judgments":"12/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_1245654658#3_2504437366","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly says plutonium-238 powered long-lived pacemaker batteries, but the document is a broad article about alpha radiation uses rather than being dedicated to plutonium's technological benefits.","learned":"Pu-238 was used in long-life nuclear pacemaker batteries, though toxicity, travel, and disposal issues ended that use."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 pacemaker batteries (+ lifespan long-lived years))","reason":"Search for a document specifically focused on plutonium-powered pacemakers or nuclear pacemaker batteries, which may provide grade-3 medical technology evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 pacemaker batteries (+ lifespan long-lived years))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1439205538#7_2881541319","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q966","rank":1,"snippet":"iodide batteries were developed which could power a pacemaker far longer than that: for five to 10 years. So the pressing need for rechargeable pacemakers diminished, while the imminent threat of lawsuits did not. Thanks to both technological advances and the legal profession, the idea of rechargeable pacemakers were quickly abandoned. It is an idea that is reconsidered by pacemaker developers every once and a while, but so far the potential risks (to the companies, at least), have outweighed the potential benefits. Why Can't They Make Pacemaker Batteries Last Much Longer Than They Do Now? The fact is, they could make pacemaker <cover>batteries that last substantially longer than they do now. In fact, in the 1960s and 1970s, a few pacemaker companies made nuclear-powered pacemakers which were powered by plutonium-238 — which has a half-life of 87 years — </cover>so these pacemakers were virtually guaranteed not to run out of \"juice\" during the lifetime of the patient.\" , \"start_char\": 5096.000000 , \"title\": \"Pacemaker Replacement Due to Low Battery\" , \"url\": \"https://www.verywell.com/pacemaker-low-battery-replacement-1746230\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"13/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1439205538#7_2881541319","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports a technological benefit of plutonium-238 by explaining that nuclear-powered pacemakers used Pu-238 with an 87-year half-life and could last for a patient's lifetime, but the article is broadly about pacemaker battery replacement rather than dedicated to plutonium's role in technology.","learned":"Pu-238 enabled very long-lived nuclear pacemakers that were unlikely to run out of power during a patient's lifetime."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q966","reason":"The pacemaker battery query is on a promising direction and may yield more focused documents about plutonium-powered pacemakers or nuclear battery medical technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q966"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1439205538#8_2881543338","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q966","rank":3,"snippet":"Now? A Word From Verywell \" , \"segment\": \"Thanks to both technological advances and the legal profession, the idea of rechargeable pacemakers were quickly abandoned. It is an idea that is reconsidered by pacemaker developers every once and a while, but so far the potential risks (to the companies, at least), have outweighed the potential benefits. Why Can't They Make Pacemaker Batteries Last Much Longer Than They Do Now? The fact is, they could make pacemaker <cover>batteries that last substantially longer than they do now. In fact, in the 1960s and 1970s, a few pacemaker companies made nuclear-powered pacemakers which were powered by plutonium-238 — which has a half-life of 87 years — </cover>so these pacemakers were virtually guaranteed not to run out of \"juice\" during the lifetime of the patient. Indeed, a few of these pacemakers may still be in operation today. But, as you might imagine, there were some obvious problems with nuclear pacemakers: first, plutonium is a highly toxic substance, and even if a minuscule amount leaks into the bloodstream, death would rapidly ensue. And because plutonium is obviously a substance of great interest to regulators (and to even the darker ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_27_1245654658#3_2504437366","rank":2,"relevance":2}]}}
