{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_1439205538#8_2881543338","relevance":2,"reason":"This is an overlapping segment from the same Verywell pacemaker battery article and directly states that Pu-238 nuclear pacemakers could last a patient's lifetime, but the document is not dedicated to plutonium's technological role and includes substantial discussion of risks and replacement issues.","learned":"Repeated pacemaker evidence: Pu-238's long half-life enabled nuclear pacemakers with lifetime-scale battery duration, though toxicity and regulation limited use."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium \"nuclear pacemaker\" (+ battery batteries powered power))","reason":"Try a more specific nuclear-pacemaker query to find documents dedicated to plutonium-powered medical device technology rather than general pacemaker battery articles."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium \"nuclear pacemaker\" (+ battery batteries powered power))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_44_499779068#6_1187482027","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q967","rank":1,"snippet":"Howard Stark’s notes on a mythical energy source, the Tesseract, he’s able to create a new element that could replace palladium. To make an Arc Reactor safe, you would either need that as-yet undiscovered element or some sort of new shielding material that could protect the human body from the effects of heavy radiation. Could a nuclear-powered heart extend someone’s life? Yes, but scientists would recommend you try something else first. Pacemakers, which stimulate a regular heartbeat when the body’s own electrical currents won’t suffice, were once <cover>powered with a radioactive material called plutonium-238. These didn’t use chain reactions to make energy, so they weren’t nuclear reactors. An nuclear pacemaker. </cover>Not quite an Arc Reactor. Los Alamos National Lab The world’s first nuclear-powered pacemaker was installed in a person in 1972, but the practice was discontinued in 1988. The problem:\" , \"start_char\": 3462.000000 , \"title\": \"How real-life technology is catching up to Iron Man's heart\" , \"url\": \"https://www.inverse.com/innovation/build-iron-mans-arc-reactor\" }"}}
