{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1152658657#0_1303710121","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to plutonium-238 and its applications, with headings specifically including use in radioisotope thermoelectric generators and nuclear-powered pacemakers; the snippet also identifies Pu-238 as an isotope used in RTGs and gives its long half-life, directly supporting how plutonium enables technology.","learned":"A dedicated Pu-238 source can support both space RTG technology and nuclear-powered pacemaker applications because of Pu-238's alpha decay and long half-life."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q969","reason":"This broader Pu-238 pacemaker/atomic battery query found a dedicated Pu-238 applications source; continue for more focused grade-3 results on nuclear pacemaker technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q969"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#16_314164194","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q969","rank":3,"snippet":"Uses of Plutonium Abundance and Isotopes References Cite this Page \" , \"segment\": \"PuO 52+ (olive green) (in sodium hydroxide). ( 5) If you were to touch a small piece of plutonium metal (please don’t!) it would feel warm because of the energy released by alpha decay. A larger piece of the metal could boil water. Uses of Plutonium Plutonium-239, which can undergo nuclear chain reactions, is used in nuclear bombs and nuclear reactors Plutonium-238 is used as a long-lived heat and power source for space probes. ( Its intrinsic heat output is approximately 0.5 watts per gram.) The Pioneer and Voyager space probes used plutonium-238 nuclear batteries as a power source. Three radioisotope heater units (each containing 2.7 grams of plutonium-238 dioxide) were used as heat sources on the Pathfinder Mars robot lander. Each radioisotope heater unit produces about one watt of heat. ( 6), (7) Early <cover>pacemaker batteries also used tiny amounts of plutonium-238.</cover>\" , \"start_char\": 5536.000000 , \"title\": \"Plutonium\" , \"url\": \"https://www.chemicool.com/elements/plutonium.html\" }"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"17/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#16_314164194","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to plutonium and its uses, and directly states that plutonium-238 is used as a long-lived heat and power source for space probes, powered Pioneer and Voyager nuclear batteries, provided heat for Mars Pathfinder, and was used in early pacemaker batteries.","learned":"Pu-238 improves technology by serving as a long-lived heat/electric power source for space probes, rover heater units, and early pacemaker batteries."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q969","reason":"The Pu-238 pacemaker/atomic battery query is producing dedicated applications sources with direct evidence; continue for additional grade-3 documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q969"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#17_314165595","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q969","rank":4,"snippet":"Department of Energy Discovery of Plutonium Dr. Doug Stewart Appearance and Characteristics Harmful effects: Characteristics: Uses of Plutonium Abundance and Isotopes References Cite this Page \" , \"segment\": \"Its intrinsic heat output is approximately 0.5 watts per gram.) The Pioneer and Voyager space probes used plutonium-238 nuclear batteries as a power source. Three radioisotope heater units (each containing 2.7 grams of plutonium-238 dioxide) were used as heat sources on the Pathfinder Mars robot lander. Each radioisotope heater unit produces about one watt of heat. ( 6), (7) Early <cover>pacemaker batteries also used tiny amounts of plutonium-238. </cover>The image on the left shows the decay of one atom of plutonium-238. This releases 5.6 million electron volts of energy. To get an idea of what this means, consider NASA’s Curiosity Mars rover, which will be powered by 4.8 kg of plutonium dioxide. During its first 87.7 year half-life, the plutonium will produce about 4800 gigajoules of energy. To generate the same energy using natural gas (mainly methane) the Mars rover would need to carry about 86 metric tons of methane and 345 metric tons of oxygen.\" ,"}}
