{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#17_314165595","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the dedicated Chemicool plutonium page, directly stating that Pu-238 powered space-probe nuclear batteries, Mars lander heater units, early pacemaker batteries, and that Curiosity's plutonium dioxide provides large amounts of energy compared with chemical fuel.","learned":"Repeated but useful evidence: Pu-238 provides very high energy density and long-lived heat/power for space and medical technologies."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ radioisotope thermoelectric generators rtg rtgs) (+ reliable continuous long-lived))","reason":"Search more generally for dedicated RTG/radioisotope power documents explaining the technological advantage of plutonium systems, likely to find additional distinct grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ radioisotope thermoelectric generators rtg rtgs) (+ reliable continuous long-lived))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1101808721#11_2078284081","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q970","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1101808721#11_2078284081\" , \"end_char\": 7327.000000 , \"headings\": \"How far can Voyager 1 go before we lose contact? How far can Voyager 1 go before we lose contact? How long we can communicate with the Voyager 1? Voyager 1 and the “Pale blue dot” Notes Sources M. Özgür Nevres \" , \"segment\": \"It uses three RTG units – (<cover>Radioisotope Thermal Generator), which converts the heat from decaying plutonium into electricity using Peltier devices. It is not very fancy, has no moving parts, and is very reliable, </cover>but it produces a lot less power than a nuclear reactor. The heliosphere is the vast, bubble-like region of space which surrounds and is created by the Sun. In plasma physics terms, this is the cavity formed by the Sun in the surrounding interstellar medium. The “bubble” of the heliosphere is continuously “inflated” by plasma originating from the Sun, known as the solar wind. Outside the heliosphere, this solar plasma gives way to the interstellar plasma permeating our galaxy. Radiation levels inside and outside the heliosphere differ; ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#8_2118930959","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
