{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_1101808721#11_2078284081","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains a technological benefit—Voyager uses RTGs that convert heat from decaying plutonium into reliable electricity with no moving parts—but the document is mainly about Voyager 1's communication range rather than being dedicated to plutonium technology.","learned":"Pu-powered RTGs improve spacecraft technology by providing reliable electricity from decay heat without moving parts."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium \"radioisotope power systems\" nasa (+ electricity heat missions))","reason":"Seek authoritative NASA/DOE pages dedicated to radioisotope power systems that explicitly explain how plutonium provides electricity and heat for missions."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium \"radioisotope power systems\" nasa (+ electricity heat missions))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#2_3181810141","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q971","rank":1,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#2_3181810141\" , \"end_char\": 2345.000000 , \"headings\": \"How to Make Plutonium How to Make Plutonium 1 RECEIVE NEPTUNIUM-237 2 ELIMINATE PROTACTINIUM 3 CREATE TARGETS 4 IRRADIATE NEPTUNIUM 5 EXTRACT TARGETS 6 SEPARATE <cover>PLUTONIUM 7 SEND TO LOS ALAMOS \" , \"segment\": \"NASA missions bound beyond the influence of the sun, where solar power isn't an option, use radioisotope power systems (</cover>RPS) to create electricity from the heat of atomic decay. RPS that use 4.8 kilograms of fuel have powered experiments on the moon and Mars and launched golden records etched with the essence of human civilization to the edge of the solar system. They run only on plutonium-238, which is (relatively) cheap, has a suitable half-life, and gets plenty hot. But it was a by-product of Cold War–era nuclear weapon factories—now decommissioned—and by the 2010s there were fewer than ten RPS' worth remaining for NASA to use. So scientists lobbied the DOE to start making it again. Tennessee's Oak Ridge National Laboratory "}}
