{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#2_3181810141","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is focused on making plutonium-238 for NASA radioisotope power systems and directly explains that missions beyond effective solar power use Pu-238 RPS to create electricity from atomic decay heat, powering Moon, Mars, and outer-solar-system missions.","learned":"Pu-238 improves space technology by enabling radioisotope power systems for missions where solar power is not an option, because it has a suitable half-life and produces useful heat."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q971","reason":"This NASA/RPS query produced a strong, distinct grade-3 result about plutonium-238 space power; continue for more authoritative documents."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q971"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#1_3181808627","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q971","rank":2,"snippet":" , \"headings\": \"How to Make Plutonium How to Make Plutonium 1 RECEIVE NEPTUNIUM-237 2 ELIMINATE PROTACTINIUM 3 CREATE TARGETS 4 IRRADIATE NEPTUNIUM 5 EXTRACT TARGETS 6 SEPARATE PLUTONIUM 7 SEND TO LOS ALAMOS \" , \"segment\": \"It's got your calcium, platinum, and titanium. Your ytterbium, your strontium-90, and of course uranium-235 and plutonium-239 (responsible for Hiroshima and Nagasaki). In this catalog, <cover>plutonium-238 is the only-on-one-model-year carburetor from the old car you've somehow kept running: useful, unique—and discontinued. And the old car is a spacecraft: NASA missions bound beyond the influence of the sun, where solar power isn't an option, use radioisotope power systems (</cover>RPS) to create electricity from the heat of atomic decay. RPS that use 4.8 kilograms of fuel have powered experiments on the moon and Mars and launched golden records etched with the essence of human civilization to the edge of the solar system. They run only on plutonium-238, which is (relatively) cheap, has a suitable half-life, and gets plenty hot. But it was a by-product of Cold War–era nuclear weapon "}}
