{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#1_3181808627","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the same focused article on making plutonium-238 for NASA, directly explaining that Pu-238 is unique and useful for spacecraft radioisotope power systems that make electricity from atomic decay heat where solar power is not an option.","learned":"Repeated evidence: Pu-238's suitable half-life and heat output make it uniquely valuable for NASA radioisotope power systems on Moon, Mars, and outer-solar-system missions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q971","reason":"The radioisotope power systems query is yielding dedicated evidence about plutonium improving space technology; continue for additional distinct results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q971"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#8_3345698713","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q971","rank":3,"snippet":"Next-Gen Mars Rover More Nuclear News \" , \"segment\": \"it will be connected to the rover for the final time on the launch pad atop the mission’s Atlas V launch vehicle in July, before the planned launch of the Mars 2020 mission. Source: NASA But Perseverance is also especially notable because it will be the first rover to use <cover>plutonium created by Oak Ridge National Laboratory. Oak Ridge is working with INL and Los Alamos national laboratory to help NASA beef up its fuel supply for Pu-238. “ Pu-238 gives off a large amount of heat due to radioactive decay during the course of its 87.7 year half-life,” INL said. While other radioisotopes have been studied and used in the past for radioisotope power systems, </cover>Pu-238 has demonstrated the best properties for conversion from heat to electricity for space missions, and it has been used safely since the 1960s. However, while the U.S. has enough plutonium to fuel the space missions scheduled for the next decade, the domestic supply of plutonium is a limited resource. In order to establish a new supply of Pu-238 to enable future NASA "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"22/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#8_3345698713","relevance":3,"reason":"The passage directly explains that Pu-238 gives off substantial heat during its 87.7-year half-life, has the best properties for converting heat to electricity for space missions, has been used safely since the 1960s, and is needed to enable future NASA missions.","learned":"Pu-238 improves space power technology because its decay heat is well suited for conversion to electricity in radioisotope power systems, enabling current and future NASA missions."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q971","reason":"The NASA/radioisotope power systems query is producing strong grade-3 evidence about plutonium's technological value; continue to gather additional direct results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q971"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1339766507#8_2726895580","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q971","rank":5,"snippet":"NASA uses low volumes of high-efficiency cells, very periodically and very specifically, so costs are higher.” NASA has taken an interest in solar for a long time. While the very first satellites were battery powered, solar arrays became common in orbit by the ’60s. Regular silicon cells were used first, until gallium arsenide made it out of R&D in the ’90s. Now, almost everything arriving in the ionosphere is multi-junction. Why does <cover>NASA use solar technology in space? While solar technology can be a political football on the ground—tossed around and tackled often—in space, it encounters little opposition. For starters, power alternatives in space include batteries and politically difficult radioisotope power systems, or RPS. RPS converts heat generated by the natural decay of the radioactive isotope plutonium-</cover>238 into electricity. Batteries have the unfortunate habit of running low on power, eventually turning a multi-million dollar spacecraft into space junk.\" , \"start_char\": 4623.000000 , \"title\": \"What kind of solar panels does NASA use?\" , \"url\": \"https://www.solarpowerworldonline.com/2016/03/kind-solar-panels-nasa-use/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","rank":4,"relevance":3}]}}
