{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ mox \"mixed oxide\") (+ reactor reactors fuel electricity))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#6_3696778256","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q973","rank":1,"snippet":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#6_3696778256\" , \"end_char\": 6126.000000 , \"headings\": \"MOX fuel MOX fuel Contents Overview Current applications Thermal reactors Fast reactors Fabrication Dry mixing Coprecipitation Americium content Curium content Thorium MOX See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"AECL reported to the United States National Academy of Sciences committee on plutonium disposition that it has extensive experience in testing the use of MOX fuel containing from 0.5 to 3% plutonium. The content of un-burnt <cover>plutonium in spent MOX fuel </cover>from thermal reactors is significant – greater than 50% of the initial plutonium loading. However, during the burning of MOX the ratio of fissile (odd numbered) isotopes to non-fissile (even) drops from around 65% to 20%, depending on burn up. This makes any attempt to recover the fissile isotopes difficult and any bulk Pu recovered would require such a high fraction of Pu in any second generation MOX that it would be impractical. This means that such a spent fuel would be difficult to reprocess for further reuse (burning) of plutonium. Regular reprocessing of biphasic spent MOX is "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"25/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#6_3696778256","relevance":2,"reason":"The document is dedicated to MOX fuel and discusses plutonium-containing reactor fuel, but this snippet focuses on spent MOX fuel and reprocessing difficulty rather than clearly explaining how plutonium improves or enables technology.","learned":"MOX fuel is a plutonium-containing reactor fuel application, but this result emphasized spent-fuel issues more than a technological benefit."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q973","reason":"The MOX fuel query is a promising diversification toward nuclear energy technology; continue for results that more directly explain plutonium's value as reactor fuel for electricity or fuel recycling."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q973"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#10_3696785392","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q973","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#10_3696785392\" , \"end_char\": 8564.000000 , \"headings\": \"MOX fuel MOX fuel Contents Overview Current applications Thermal reactors Fast reactors Fabrication Dry mixing Coprecipitation Americium content Curium content Thorium MOX See also References External links \" , \"segment\": \"Of the total nuclear fuel used today, MOX provides 2%. Licensing and safety issues of using <cover>MOX fuel include: As plutonium </cover>isotopes absorb more neutrons than uranium fuels, reactor control systems may need modification. MOX fuel tends to run hotter because of lower thermal conductivity, which may be an issue in some reactor designs. Fission gas release in MOX fuel assemblies may limit the maximum burn-up time of MOX fuel. About 30% of the plutonium originally loaded into MOX fuel is consumed by use in a thermal reactor. In theory, if one third of the core fuel load is MOX and two-thirds uranium fuel, there is zero net gain of plutonium in the spent fuel, and the cycle could be repeated; however, there remains multiple difficulties in reprocessing spent MOX fuel. As of "}}
