{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_17_3606870982#10_3696785392","relevance":2,"reason":"The document is dedicated to MOX fuel and directly discusses plutonium in reactor fuel, including that some plutonium is consumed in thermal reactors and MOX supplies a small share of nuclear fuel, but this segment emphasizes licensing, safety, and reprocessing difficulties more than a clear technological benefit.","learned":"Plutonium can be used in MOX reactor fuel, where part of the plutonium is consumed for nuclear energy production, but MOX use can require reactor modifications and has fuel-cycle challenges."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q973","reason":"Continue the MOX fuel result list to look for a clearer overview passage explaining plutonium's benefit in reactor fuel, energy production, fuel recycling, or reducing weapons plutonium stockpiles."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q973"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1127020710#9_2028452574","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q973","rank":3,"snippet":"fission Example - Neutron flux in a MOX fueled thermal reactor core Mixed oxide fuel, commonly referred to as MOX fuel, is nuclear fuel that contains more than one oxide of fissile material. MOX fuels usually consist of plutonium blended with natural uranium. For simplicity and for purposes of this example, it will be assumed, that the reactor core contains only MOX fuel (100% MOX) and that the averaged percentage of plutonium (averaged over all the used fuel assemblies – not all FAs are fresh FAs) in MOX fuel is equal to 4%. Note that, the initial percentage of <cover>plutonium in fresh MOX fuel </cover>is around ~7%. Moreover, it will be assumed, the recycled plutonium contains only the fissile 239 Pu. In reality, MOX fuel always contain significant amounts of higher isotopes – 240 Pu, 241 Pu and 242 Pu. The presence of these amounts will be neglected in this example. A typical thermal reactor contains about 100 tons of fuel (HM – heavy metal).\" , \"start_char\": 4646.000000 , \"title\": \"Neutron Flux Density - Neutron Intensity | Definition | nuclear-power.net\" , \"url\": \"https://www.nuclear-power.net/nuclear-"}}
{"request":{"judgments":"27/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1127020710#9_2028452574","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage mentions plutonium in MOX fuel, but the document is mainly about neutron flux density and uses MOX only as a reactor-physics example; it does not directly answer how plutonium improves technology.","learned":"MOX fuel can contain several percent plutonium, but this result is not focused on plutonium's technological benefits."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ applications uses) (+ space probes pacemakers reactors batteries))","reason":"Search for documents dedicated to plutonium applications across space power, medical batteries, and nuclear reactors, which are likely to provide direct grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ applications uses) (+ space probes pacemakers reactors batteries))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1256106727#13_2266413751","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q974","rank":1,"snippet":"ELIMINATING URANIUM AND PLUTONIUM? “Thorium is considered by some as the nuclear fuel of the future.” INDIA LEADS THE WAY THORIUM – REACHING AROUND THE WORLD “After India, the main countries driving thorium research are America and Russia.” WHEN WILL THORIUM MAKE AN IMPRESSION? THE FUTURE FOR THORIUM \" , \"segment\": \"India has a three-stage plan for nuclear energy. Stage one uses uranium-fuelled PHWRs (pressurised heavy water reactors) and LWRs to produce plutonium. In stage two this plutonium-based fuel is used in FBRs to breed U-233, and more <cover>plutonium and thorium. Stage three uses advanced heavy water reactors </cover>to burn the U-233 and Pu-239 with thorium. The thorium will produce around 75% of the power, with the spent fuel being reprocessed to recycle fissile materials. “Thorium is a much safer fuel source than uranium or plutonium.” India views ADS as a definite contender for power in the near term and for incinerating long-lived radioactive waste from the uranium fuel cycle. It has been studying all aspects of the ADS subsystems and work is continuing on improvements to beam current from the driver accelerator and improve thermal hydraulics and materials compatibility. "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"28/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1256106727#13_2266413751","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage mentions plutonium-based fuel in India's nuclear energy/thorium cycle, but it is primarily about thorium and advanced reactors, not dedicated to how plutonium improves technology.","learned":"Broad applications queries including reactors can drift into thorium fuel-cycle discussions rather than focused plutonium technology evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ applications uses) (+ spacecraft pacemakers \"nuclear batteries\" \"space probes\"))","reason":"Refocus on dedicated plutonium applications sources likely to summarize direct technology benefits such as spacecraft power, nuclear batteries, and pacemakers."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ applications uses) (+ spacecraft pacemakers \"nuclear batteries\" \"space probes\"))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_798058821#11_1716771796","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q975","rank":2,"snippet":"\" , \"segment\": \"They are used to provide thermoelectric cooling however the efficiencies of Peltier effect devices are typically around 5% to 10%, much less than the 40% to 50% achievable with compressor based refrigeration units which limits their use to small portable refrigerators and cooling plates. Seebeck effect thermopiles are used to convert heat energy into electrical energy in thermoelectric generators (TEGs) with electrical power outputs of 1000 Watts or more. TEGs have been used for some time in Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generators (RTGs) to provide portable power in <cover>spacecraft applications using the heat from the decay of radioactive isotopes such as Plutonium </cover>238. See Nuclear Batteries . More recently the possibility of using thermocouple arrays in automotive applications to recover waste heat from engine exhaust gases is being investigated. With an exhaust gas temperature of 250°C and a coolant temperature of 50°C, power outputs of over 300 Watts have been achieved but this drops to 150 Watts when the coolant temperature increases to 90° C See also Direct Conversion of Heat Energy to Electrical Energy (2) the AMTEC Thermal Battery Return to Electrical Energy Overview\" , \"start_char\"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1152658657#0_1303710121","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"29/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_47_798058821#11_1716771796","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly supports one answer by saying RTGs provide portable spacecraft power using heat from radioactive isotope decay such as plutonium-238, but the document is mainly about thermoelectric/Seebeck devices and energy conversion rather than plutonium's technology benefits.","learned":"Pu-238 decay heat can be converted by thermoelectric generators into portable spacecraft power, but broad thermoelectric queries may retrieve only partial evidence."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ \"deep space\" spacecraft missions) (+ \"solar power\" sunlight insufficient))","reason":"Search for documents that directly explain the technological advantage of Pu-238 in deep space, where solar power is inadequate; this has been the strongest grade-3 evidence direction."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ \"deep space\" spacecraft missions) (+ \"solar power\" sunlight insufficient))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_839214813#12_1795875815","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q976","rank":2,"snippet":"missions to Saturn. So it could be a long time before NASA can finally further explore the tiny moon. Read More From Heavy Ed Roberts, Activist: 5 Fast Facts You Need to Know 4. Cassini Has to Use Plutonium as a Power Source Because Saturn Is Too Far From the Sun This image shows the moon Pan between a gap in Saturn’s rings. ( NASA/JPL-Caltech/Space Science Institute) Cassini – and other satellites NASA has sent into <cover>deep space – cannot use solar power as a power source because it is traveling too far from the sun. Instead, it uses three radioisotope thermoelectric generators that are heated from the decay of about 73 lbs (33 kg) of plutonium, specifically plutonium-238. </cover>The substance is a byproduct of the process to make nuclear weapons. Back in 1997, the Christian Science Monitor reported that there were protests at the time to stop NASA from hurtling Cassini into space. It was the largest single amount of plutonium ever used by a space device and some U.S. lawmakers called for the Cassini mission to be scrapped.\" , \"start_char\": 6921.000000 , \"title\": \"","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#1_3181808627","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"30/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_21_839214813#12_1795875815","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains that Cassini and other deep-space NASA spacecraft use plutonium-238 RTGs because they are too far from the Sun for solar power, but the document appears to be a broader Cassini/Saturn article rather than dedicated to plutonium's technological benefits.","learned":"Pu-238 RTGs enabled Cassini's Saturn mission by providing power where solar power was inadequate."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ uses applications) (+ \"space probes\" pacemakers batteries power))","reason":"Search for dedicated plutonium-use overview pages that directly summarize technology benefits such as space-probe power and pacemaker/nuclear batteries, likely yielding more grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ uses applications) (+ \"space probes\" pacemakers batteries power))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_145214343#8_316451218","ok":true,"op":"query","qid":"q977","rank":1,"snippet":"Reaction Plutonium-239 Uses Plutonium-239 Contamination and Health Risks Pu-239 Alpha Radiation Effects Pu-239 Effects in Human Body References: \" , \"segment\": \"When a slow neutron is absorbed by this isotope, the fissile reaction produces more than two neutrons. A continuous chain reaction is initiated in case one of these resultant neutrons breaks down another plutonium nucleus. Plutonium-239 Uses Plutonium-239 with high levels of purity can be produced in large quantities. Due to its properties, it is extensively used in nuclear <cover>power reactors and in the production of nuclear weapons. The various uses of Pu-239 are discussed below: Plutonium-</cover>239 gathered from dismounted nuclear weapons as well as from nuclear reactors is an important source of energy to generate electricity. Almost around 10 million kilowatt-hours of electricity are produced by one kilogram of Pu-239. Almost one-third of the energy that is produced in a commercial atomic power plant comes from plutonium fissions. The fission reaction of Plutonium-239 can be held in the same fuel rods where it is produced. Small portions of Pu-239 can be added to newly-manufactured nuclear fuel to produce MOX fuel "}}
