{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_18_1152658657#2_1303714921","relevance":3,"reason":"This is another segment of the dedicated Plutonium-238 document already seen; although the snippet is mostly headings/history, the document is focused on Pu-238 and explicitly includes sections on use in radioisotope thermoelectric generators and nuclear-powered pacemakers, directly supporting plutonium's technological uses.","learned":"Repeated Pu-238 overview evidence: the isotope is documented as useful for RTGs and nuclear-powered pacemakers."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ doe nasa) (+ \"radioisotope power systems\" \"radioisotope thermoelectric generators\") (+ missions electricity heat))","reason":"Find more authoritative DOE/NASA evidence dedicated to Pu-238 radioisotope power systems, with direct discussion of electricity and heat for missions."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 (+ doe nasa) (+ \"radioisotope power systems\" \"radioisotope thermoelectric generators\") (+ missions electricity heat))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#10_314158031","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q979","rank":2,"snippet":"\"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#10_314158031\" , \"end_char\": 4628.000000 , \"headings\": \"Plutonium Element Facts Plutonium Element Facts Pu Data Zone A plutonium ‘button.’ Photo: Department of Energy Discovery of Plutonium Dr. Doug Stewart Appearance and Characteristics Harmful effects: Characteristics: Uses of Plutonium Abundance and Isotopes References Cite this Page \" , \"segment\": \"Radioactive decay of 10.6 pounds (4.8 kilograms) of plutonium dioxide has produced a steady flow of heat to warm the rover’s systems during the intensely cold Martian night and allowed electricity to be generated. Image: NASA. The Voyager 2 spacecraft, launched on Aug. 20, 1977, is about 17 billion kilometers (10 billion miles) from the sun. It is the longest continuously operating NASA spacecraft. It owes its long life to <cover>radioisotope thermoelectric generators; these generate electricity from heat flowing from plutonium-238’s radioactive decay. Image: NASA. </cover>Radioactive decay of plutonium-238.\" , \"start_char\": 4028.000000 , \"title\": \"Plutonium\" , \"url\": \"https://www.chemicool.com/elements/plutonium.html\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1526070640#1_3181808627","rank":1,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"36/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_144201799#10_314158031","relevance":3,"reason":"This dedicated plutonium element facts page directly explains that plutonium dioxide decay provides steady heat to warm Mars rover systems and allows electricity generation, and that Voyager 2 owes its long operating life to RTGs generating electricity from plutonium-238 decay heat.","learned":"Pu-238 improves space technology by giving rovers heat/electricity and enabling exceptionally long-lived spacecraft power through RTGs."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q979","reason":"The DOE/NASA Pu-238 radioisotope power query is yielding direct grade-3 evidence from application-focused sources; continue for more authoritative results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q979"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_664873627#0_1353834332","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q979","rank":6,"snippet":"And even though they’re 18 billion km (11 billion miles) from the Sun, they’re still valuable scientifically. But they’re running out of energy, and if NASA wants them to continue on much longer, they have some decisions to make. The Energy Problem The energy issue is becoming more and more critical over time for the Voyagers. Not only do their scientific instruments require energy, but the spacecraft need to keep themselves warm in the frigid environment of space. The pair of spacecraft aren’t solar powered: that wouldn’t be possible so far from the Sun. They rely on <cover>radioisotope thermoelectric generators (RTG) for their energy. Each of the Voyager probes has three RTGs, and they use plutonium 238 for their fuel source. As that isotope decays, it produces heat which is converted to electrical energy.\" , \"start_char\": 0.000000 , \"title\": \"NASA </cover>has Figured Out How to Extend the Lives of the Voyagers Even Longer - Universe Today\" , \"url\": \"https://www.universetoday.com/142802/nasa-has-figured-out-how-to-extend-the-lives-of-the-voyagers-even-longer/\" }","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","rank":3,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#8_3345698713","rank":4,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","rank":5,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"37/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_56_664873627#0_1353834332","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains that the Voyager probes rely on plutonium-238 RTGs for heat converted into electrical energy because solar power is not possible so far from the Sun, but the article is mainly about extending the Voyager spacecraft missions rather than being dedicated to plutonium's technological benefits.","learned":"Pu-238 RTGs enable long-lived deep-space spacecraft by supplying both warmth and electricity where solar power cannot work."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ \"what is plutonium used for\" uses applications) (+ spacecraft pacemakers reactors))","reason":"Look for general plutonium-use overview pages dedicated to applications, likely to provide direct grade-3 evidence across space power, pacemakers, and reactor technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium (+ \"what is plutonium used for\" uses applications) (+ spacecraft pacemakers reactors))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#8_757027388","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q980","rank":3,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#8_757027388\" , \"end_char\": 4554.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Is Plutonium? What Is Plutonium?<cover>What Is Plutonium Used For? Role of Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors</cover>Where Is Plutonium Found on Earth? How Many Neutrons Does Plutonium Have? Management of Recovered Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors Health Hazard Environmental Effects References \" , \"segment\": \"The closed cycle consists in subjecting the spent fuel to a mechanical-chemical process, known as reprocessing or reprocessing, which allows the separation of uranium and plutonium that still contain fission and transuranic products. The recovered uranium and plutonium are used to make new fuel and fission and transuranic products constitute the high-activity nuclear waste. How Many Neutrons Does Plutonium Have? Plutonium can have 238 or 239 neutrons. The isotope with the highest chemical interest is plutonium-239. It is formed in nuclear reactors. It is a fissile isotope but can also capture neutrons to form superior isotopes. In the field of nuclear energy, plutionio-239 is used as a nuclear fuel in the production of radioactive isotopes for research ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_08_1256106727#13_2266413751","rank":1,"relevance":1},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_36_145214343#8_316451218","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
{"request":{"judgments":"38/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#8_757027388","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to plutonium and its uses, with sections on what plutonium is used for and its role in nuclear reactors; it directly explains that recovered plutonium can be made into new fuel and that plutonium-239 is used as nuclear fuel in nuclear energy technology.","learned":"Plutonium helps nuclear technology by enabling fuel recycling/reprocessing and serving as fissile nuclear fuel in reactors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q980","reason":"This general plutonium-use query is yielding dedicated application overview evidence; continue to find more direct grade-3 results on reactor, spacecraft, or pacemaker uses."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q980"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#9_757029127","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q980","rank":4,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_25_386750115#9_757029127\" , \"end_char\": 5120.000000 , \"headings\": \"What Is Plutonium? What Is Plutonium?<cover>What Is Plutonium Used For? Role of Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors</cover>Where Is Plutonium Found on Earth? How Many Neutrons Does Plutonium Have? Management of Recovered Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors Health Hazard Environmental Effects References \" , \"segment\": \"It is formed in nuclear reactors. It is a fissile isotope but can also capture neutrons to form superior isotopes. In the field of nuclear energy, plutionio-239 is used as a nuclear fuel in the production of radioactive isotopes for research and as a fissile agent in nuclear weapons. Plutonium-238 is used in heat sources for space applications, thermoelectric heat generators and has been used in cardiac pacemakers. An important characteristic of many of the isotopes of plutonium is that they exhibit the phenomenon of spontaneous fission, in which the atomic nucleus can be divided spontaneously without the need to be bombarded by any neutron. Management of Recovered Plutonium in Nuclear Reactors In spent fuel, between 7 and 8 "}}
