{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_34_1622416910#1_3408167182","relevance":3,"reason":"The Britannica document is dedicated to plutonium-239 and directly identifies it as a fissile material of great importance in nuclear power, supporting plutonium's role in energy technology.","learned":"Plutonium-239 helps nuclear technology because it is an important fissile material for nuclear power, produced from uranium-238 in reactors."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q981","reason":"The plutonium-239 reactor fuel query produced a strong authoritative result on nuclear power; continue to find more direct evidence about plutonium's energy-technology benefits."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q981"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1125768141#0_2026051558","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q981","rank":2,"snippet":"{ \"docid\": \"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1125768141#0_2026051558\" , \"end_char\": 1154.000000 , \"headings\": \"Plutonium 239 Plutonium 239 Plutonium 239 Fission See previous: \" , \"segment\": \"<cover>Plutonium 239 | Fission & Properties | nuclear-</cover>power.net Plutonium 239 Plutonium 239, 239Pu, is a fissile isotope, which means 239 Pu is capable of undergoing fission reaction after absorbing thermal neutron. Moreover, 239 Pu meets also alternative requirement that the amount ( ~2.88 per one fission by thermal neutron) of neutrons produced by fission of 239 Pu is sufficient to sustain a nuclear fission chain reaction. This isotope is the principal fissile isotope of plutonium in use. It is a man-made isotope and can be found in an irradiated uranium fuel or in a spent uranium fuel. Isotope 239 Pu is formed in a nuclear reactor from fertile isotope 238 U, which constitute more than 95% of uranium fuel (e.g. PWRs and BWRs require 3% – 5% of 235 U). Absorption of a resonance or thermal neutron by the 238 U nucleus yields 239 U. The half-life of 239 U is approximately "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"42/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_48_1125768141#0_2026051558","relevance":3,"reason":"The document is dedicated to plutonium-239 and directly explains that Pu-239 is a fissile isotope capable of sustaining a nuclear fission chain reaction, is the principal fissile isotope of plutonium in use, and is formed in nuclear reactors from uranium-238, supporting plutonium's role in nuclear power technology.","learned":"Pu-239 improves nuclear energy technology because it can sustain fission chain reactions and functions as a major fissile material in reactor fuel cycles."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q981","reason":"The plutonium-239 reactor fuel query is producing dedicated, direct evidence about plutonium's role in nuclear power technology; continue for more grade-3 results."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q981"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1204386146#12_2026460924","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q981","rank":3,"snippet":"doc_02_1204386146#12_2026460924\" , \"end_char\": 8900.000000 , \"headings\": \"Nuclear energy Nuclear energy Contents Types of nuclear energy What it can do Why some people do not like it Notes References \" , \"segment\": \"A proposed waste-storage facility at Yucca Mountain has yet to be built, and few new reactors have been built in the US in recent decades. Spent fuel can be reprocessed to extract fissionable material, but this raises security and proliferation concerns; much reprocessed uranium <cover>fuel is plutonium-239 created during the fission </cover>process, which is far more readily useful for building small nuclear weapons than uranium (it is also harder to fission plutonium-239 in a thermal-spectrum reactor feasibly than it is to fission uranium-235 or uranium-233 in a thermal-spectrum reactor; for this reason, most reactors that consume plutonium-239 are fast-spectrum reactors, which tend more towards having a \"twitchy\" control response than most thermal-spectrum reactors). Even with fuel reprocessing, however, the problem of disposal of support materials (contaminated gear and the like) remains. In the United States, new construction licenses were "}}
{"request":{"judgments":"43/50"}}
{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1204386146#12_2026460924","relevance":1,"reason":"The passage mentions plutonium-239 in reprocessed nuclear fuel, but the document is broadly about nuclear energy and focuses on waste/proliferation concerns rather than directly explaining how plutonium improves technology.","learned":"Some nuclear-energy pages mention Pu-239 fuel recycling but may emphasize risks and reactor limitations rather than technological benefits."},"action":{"op":"next","qid":"q981","reason":"The plutonium-239 reactor fuel query has already produced strong grade-3 evidence; continue to look for more dedicated documents on plutonium as fissile fuel for nuclear power technology."}}
{"request":{"op":"next","qid":"q981"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_02_1204386146#11_2026458884","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q981","rank":4,"snippet":"the technology of fission reactors is sometimes prone to problems. Although waste is small in volume (compared to most industries), safe waste disposal is a tremendous problem, demanding answers that will allow the waste to stay stored for tens of thousands of years — substantially longer than all of current recorded history. A proposed waste-storage facility at Yucca Mountain has yet to be built, and few new reactors have been built in the US in recent decades. Spent fuel can be reprocessed to extract fissionable material, but this raises security and proliferation concerns; much reprocessed uranium <cover>fuel is plutonium-239 created during the fission </cover>process, which is far more readily useful for building small nuclear weapons than uranium (it is also harder to fission plutonium-239 in a thermal-spectrum reactor feasibly than it is to fission uranium-235 or uranium-233 in a thermal-spectrum reactor; for this reason, most reactors that consume plutonium-239 are fast-spectrum reactors, which tend more towards having a \"twitchy\" control response than most thermal-spectrum reactors). Even with fuel reprocessing, however, the problem of disposal of support materials (contaminated gear and the like) remains.\" , \"start_char\": "}}
