{"latest_judgment":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_52_1485404031#0_3017801629","relevance":2,"reason":"The passage directly explains that Curiosity's RTG uses heat from plutonium-238 decay to provide about 110 watts of electricity for instruments, wheels, computers, radio, and excess heat for rover systems, but the document is mainly an answer about rover mission lifetime rather than dedicated to plutonium-enabled technology.","learned":"Pu-238 improves Mars rover technology by powering instruments, mobility, computing, communications, and thermal control through radioisotope generators."},"action":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 mmrtg (+ curiosity perseverance rover) (+ electricity heat nasa))","reason":"Search more directly for NASA/MMRTG rover pages that should be dedicated to plutonium-238 power systems and contain grade-3 evidence."}}
{"request":{"op":"query","query":"(^ plutonium 238 mmrtg (+ curiosity perseverance rover) (+ electricity heat nasa))"},"response":{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_45_175984072#2_393082779","ok":true,"op":"next","qid":"q984","rank":3,"snippet":"which uses radioactive pellets to generate electricity. MARS MASTER TOUGHEST LANDING YET LOOKING FOR SIGNS OF LIFE ROUND-TRIP TICKET COVID-19 PRECAUTIONS \" , \"segment\": \"Kelly Lively, an Idaho National Laboratory Department Manager for Radio Isotope Power Systems, worked on a part of Perseverance with her team. \\\"This mission goes back to Mars and will actually go to a different location, Jezero crater, and collect rock samples along the way and cache them in stainless steel tubes,\\\" she said. The Perseverance <cover>rover will be powered by INL's Multi-Mission Radioisotope Thermoelectric Generator, or MMRTG, which uses pellet forms of the radioactive material plutonium-238 to generate electricity. \"</cover>It generates about 125 watts of electrical power and it actually charges an ion lithium battery that is onboard the rover,\" Lively explained. \" The ion lithium battery is what provides enough power at the right current to be able to power all the instruments and mobility of the platform.\" Lively and her team at INL have been working on the MMRTG for the last five years. Watch NASA's live stream of the rover's landing below or on KTVB's YouTube Channel. The rover named Perseverance ","skipped":[{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_49_1599463300#2_3345687718","rank":1,"relevance":3},{"docno":"msmarco_v2.1_doc_22_944649975#6_2118925162","rank":2,"relevance":3}]}}
